      "title": "Democracy in Ancient Athens",
      "text": "Democracy in Ancient Athens\n\nThe political system that emerged in Athens during the fifth century BCE is often described as the first democracy, though it differed sharply from modern representative government. Athenian citizens did not elect legislators to act on their behalf. Instead, they governed directly, gathering perhaps forty times a year on a hillside called the Pnyx to debate and vote on questions of war, taxation, public building, and diplomacy. Any citizen present could speak, and decisions were reached by a show of hands.\n\nThe scope of this participation, however, was narrow. Citizenship was restricted to adult males born of Athenian parents. Women, resident foreigners, and the large enslaved population were excluded entirely, which means that the enfranchised group probably amounted to no more than a sixth of the people living in the city and its surrounding territory. Modern historians therefore treat Athenian democracy as a system that combined unusually broad participation among citizens with unusually rigid boundaries around who counted as one.\n\nCentral to the system was the principle that political office should not become the property of the skilled or the wealthy. Most administrative positions were filled by lot rather than by election, on the reasoning that any citizen was competent to serve and that selection by chance prevented the formation of a permanent governing class. The Council of Five Hundred, which prepared business for the assembly, was chosen this way, its members serving for a single year. Only a few posts demanding technical expertise, notably military commands and financial supervision, remained elective.\n\nPayment for public service reinforced these arrangements. From the middle of the fifth century, jurors and later assembly attendees received a modest daily wage, allowing poorer men to take time away from their fields and workshops. Critics in antiquity, including Plato, argued that such measures encouraged the uninformed to meddle in matters they did not understand. Defenders replied that ordinary citizens, deliberating together, judged public affairs more soundly than any narrow elite could.",
