        "title": "Why Deserts Form",
        "text": "Why Deserts Form\n\nDeserts are usually defined not by heat but by scarcity: a region qualifies as a desert when it receives less than about 250 millimeters of precipitation a year, and when evaporation removes more moisture than rainfall delivers. By this standard, parts of Antarctica are deserts, as are the gravel plains of central Asia. The common ingredient is not temperature but a persistent shortage of the rising, cooling air that produces rain.\n\nThe largest deserts owe their existence to global circulation. Intense solar heating near the equator lifts moist air, which cools and sheds its water as tropical rain. The now dry air travels poleward at high altitude and descends near thirty degrees north and south. Descending air warms as it is compressed, and warm air holds more water vapor than cold air, so instead of condensing out its moisture the sinking air can take up more. The Sahara, the Arabian Desert, and the Australian interior all lie within these subtropical belts of descent.\n\nA second mechanism operates at smaller scales. When moist winds meet a mountain range, they are forced upward, cool, and drop their water on the windward slope. The air that crosses the summit and sinks on the far side is depleted, and the land below lies in what geographers call a rain shadow. The Atacama in South America and the Great Basin of North America both owe much of their aridity to barriers of this kind, though the Atacama is further dried by a cold offshore current that chills the air above it and discourages the formation of rain clouds.\n\nDistance from the ocean matters as well. Air masses moving across a broad continent lose moisture steadily, so interiors far from any coast, such as the Gobi, receive little rain even without a mountain barrier. Because these causes frequently overlap, most major deserts are the product of several processes acting together rather than of any single one.",
